mathematics is a little bit like art and  a little bit like science and one of the  things that I really care about is  allowing people to kind of access both  elements of mathematics so on the one  hand we've got this beautiful structure  that can sometimes describe the world  and sometimes describe something that  we're thinking about that doesn't have a  representation in the real world and on  the other hand we have this powerful set  of tools that come from mathematics to  describe things that we see information  that we process when people get access  to that quantitative literacy they  become powerful citizens they can change  the way they think they can vote for  things they can advocate for things that  can make decisions about their own lives  in ways that are for informed by  quantitative information so if we don't  have information available to us we make  poor decisions and it's why we as a  society think it's really important that  people know how to read and for the same  reason we think it's really important  that people have access to quantitative  literacy so they can make informed  decisions about anything involving  quantitative information our medical  care our finances and that also includes  decisions that we make as a society  about our government in our health care  and our tax system so without  quantitative literacy we're driving in  the dark and with quantitative literacy  we are powerful advocates for ourselves  and for the values that we believe in  and I think as part of that we also get  a little access to the joy and the  beauty of mathematics
 

